.TH "MKCD" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
mkcd \- make a folder with its parents, and move into it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
mkcd [--] folder
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBmkcd\fR does in one step what you would otherwise type as \fBmkdir \-p folder && cd folder\fR. It makes the folder, together with every missing folder above it, and moves your shell into it.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ mkcd ~/lab/kind/manifests
$ pwd
/home/me/lab/kind/manifests
.fi
.RE
.PP
When the folder already exists, \fBmkcd\fR just moves into it. It never empties, replaces or changes a folder that is already there.
.SS Why it is a shell function
.PP
Every program runs in its own process, and a process can only change its own current folder. A script called \fBmkcd\fR could make the folder and move into it, but your shell would stay where it was the moment the script ended. So \fBmkcd\fR is a shell function. It runs inside your shell and moves the shell itself.
.PP
That is also why \fBmkcd\fR has no file in \fBbin/\fR. It lives in \fBshell/functions.sh\fR, with \fBup\fR, and your shell loads that file when it starts.
.SS Turning it on
.PP
Run this once:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ toolbelt shell enable functions
.fi
.RE
.PP
That adds the \fBfunctions\fR setting to \fB~/.config/toolbelt/shell\fR and makes sure \fB~/.bashrc\fR or \fB~/.zshrc\fR loads toolbelt's \fBshell/init.sh\fR. Open a new terminal, or run \fBexec $SHELL\fR, and \fBmkcd\fR is there:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ type mkcd
mkcd is a function
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBtoolbelt shell disable functions\fR turns it off again.
.SS How it treats the name
.IP \(bu 2
A name with no \fB/\fR at the start is inside the current folder. \fBmkcd\fR puts \fB./\fR in front of it before it runs \fBcd\fR, so a \fBCDPATH\fR you have set never sends you to a folder of the same name somewhere else.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~\fR, \fB$HOME\fR and globs are expanded by your shell before \fBmkcd\fR sees them, as with any command.
.IP \(bu 2
A name that starts with a dash goes after \fB\-\-\fR: \fBmkcd \-\- \-drafts\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
When a file, not a folder, has the name already, \fBmkcd\fR stops with an error and changes nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
When \fBmkdir\fR fails, such as for a folder you have no rights to write in, \fBmkcd\fR stops and you stay where you were.
.PP
\fBcd\fR keeps the folder you left in \fBOLDPWD\fR, so \fBcd \-\fR takes you back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-\fR
The next word is the folder, even when it starts with a dash.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
None. \fBmkcd\fR runs \fBmkdir \-p\fR and \fBcd\fR.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
bash or zsh, and \fBmkdir\fR from coreutils. \fBshell/functions.sh\fR uses only syntax both shells share, so the same file works in either.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A new project folder
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ mkcd ~/lab/kind/manifests
$ pwd
/home/me/lab/kind/manifests
.fi
.RE
.SS A folder that exists already
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ mkcd /tmp
$ pwd
/tmp
.fi
.RE
.SS A name that starts with a dash
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ mkcd -- -drafts
$ pwd
/home/me/-drafts
.fi
.RE
.SS A file is in the way
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ touch notes
$ mkcd notes
mkcd: notes exists and is not a folder
.fi
.RE
.SS No rights to write
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ mkcd /opt/tools
mkdir: cannot create directory '/opt/tools': Permission denied
mkcd: cannot make /opt/tools
.fi
.RE
.SS Check that it is loaded
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ type mkcd
mkcd is a function
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
\fBmkcd: command not found\fR : The functions are off, or this shell started before you turned them on. Run \fBtoolbelt shell enable functions\fR, then open a new terminal.
.PP
\fBmkcd: give one folder name\fR : \fBmkcd\fR takes exactly one folder. Quote a name with spaces: \fBmkcd "my project"\fR.
.PP
\fBsudo mkcd\fR does not work : \fBsudo\fR runs programs, and \fBmkcd\fR is a function in your shell. Make the folder with \fBsudo mkdir \-p\fR, then \fBcd\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The folder exists and the shell is in it.
.TP
1
The folder could not be made or entered.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as no folder or two folders.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBup\fR, \fBtoolbelt\fR, \fBmkdir(1)\fR
